== Definition and Physical Meaning ==

From [[ψ Constitutive Relations]] (B1), the ψ permittivity is defined by:

:<math>D_\psi = \varepsilon_\psi\,\psi, \qquad \varepsilon_\psi \equiv \varepsilon_0(1 + \chi_\psi)</math>

where <math>D_\psi</math> is the ψ displacement field and <math>\chi_\psi</math> is the ψ susceptibility. The physical content:

* <math>\varepsilon_\psi = \varepsilon_0</math>: null material — ψ field passes through unmodified.
* <math>\varepsilon_\psi > \varepsilon_0</math>: material stores ψ energy — ψ wave slows, impedance drops.
* <math>\varepsilon_\psi \gg \varepsilon_0</math>: high-storage material — strongly slows ψ, low impedance, high effective index.

=== Energy Storage Density ===

The ψ field energy density stored in a material with permittivity <math>\varepsilon_\psi</math>:

:<math>u_{\psi,\rm stored} = \frac{1}{2}\varepsilon_\psi\,|\psi|^2</math>

Compared to the kinetic (temporal gradient) energy density <math>\frac{1}{2}(\partial_t\psi)^2</math> from [[ψ Conservation Laws]] (A2): high-ε_ψ material preferentially stores potential energy relative to kinetic, analogous to a capacitor storing electric potential energy rather than radiating it.

=== Connection to ψ Wave Speed ===

From the modified wave equation in B1, ψ waves inside the composite travel at:

:<math>v_\psi = \frac{1}{\sqrt{\varepsilon_\psi\,\mu_\psi}}</math>

For a composite with <math>\varepsilon_\psi = 4\varepsilon_0</math> and <math>\mu_\psi = \mu_0</math>:

:<math>v_\psi = \frac{c}{\sqrt{4}} = \frac{c}{2}</math>

ψ waves travel at half the speed of light inside this composite. The resonant frequency of the [[Psi Stabilizer]] cavity consequently shifts — the mode eigenvalue <math>k_{010}</math> is unchanged by the material (it is set by geometry), but <math>\omega_{010} = v_\psi k_{010}</math> decreases with increasing ε_ψ. This is the mechanism by which the BST bias voltage tunes the resonant frequency.

=== Connection to ψ Impedance ===

From [[ψ Impedance Theory]] (A4):

:<math>Z_\psi = \frac{1}{\omega^2}\sqrt{\frac{\mu_\psi}{\varepsilon_\psi}}</math>

High ε_ψ → low Z_ψ. The biological operator's cortical tissue has some impedance <math>Z_{\rm bio}</math>. If the device shell has ε_ψ tuned so that <math>Z_\psi^{\rm (device)} = Z_{\rm bio}^*</math> (complex conjugate match), maximum ψ power transfers from device to operator. The BST bias voltage is the tuning knob for this match.

== Complex Permittivity and Dispersion ==

In a real material, ε_ψ is complex and frequency-dependent:

:<math>\varepsilon_\psi(\omega) = \varepsilon'_\psi(\omega) - i\varepsilon''_\psi(\omega)</math>

The imaginary part <math>\varepsilon''_\psi > 0</math> represents ψ energy dissipation. The Kramers–Kronig relations (from B1) constrain the relationship between real and imaginary parts:

:<math>\varepsilon'_\psi(\omega) - \varepsilon_0 = \frac{2}{\pi}\mathcal{P}\int_0^\infty \frac{\omega'\varepsilon''_\psi(\omega')}{\omega'^2 - \omega^2}\,d\omega'</math>

For the [[Psi Stabilizer]] operating at 40 kHz, the relevant quantity is <math>\varepsilon'_\psi(40\,\text{kHz})</math> — the real (storage) component at the target frequency.

=== Debye Relaxation Model ===

For a material with a single relaxation time <math>\tau_\psi</math>:

:<math>\varepsilon_\psi(\omega) = \varepsilon_{\psi,\infty} + \frac{\varepsilon_{\psi,s} - \varepsilon_{\psi,\infty}}{1 + i\omega\tau_\psi}</math>

where <math>\varepsilon_{\psi,s}</math> is the static (DC) permittivity and <math>\varepsilon_{\psi,\infty}</math> is the high-frequency limit. This model is useful for fitting measured <math>\varepsilon_\psi(\omega)</math> data to extract the relaxation time — which characterises how quickly the material can respond to changes in ψ field amplitude (relevant for the [[Anchor Controller]] update rate).

== BST as the Primary High-ε_ψ Engineering Material ==

=== Why BST ===

Barium strontium titanate (Ba<sub>0.6</sub>Sr<sub>0.4</sub>TiO<sub>3</sub>) is selected as the high-ε_ψ phase of the [[Smart Composite Fill]] for three reasons directly traceable to the engineering requirements:

# '''High base permittivity:''' <math>\varepsilon_r(0\,\text{V}) \geq 2000</math> at the Curie temperature — orders of magnitude above conventional dielectrics. Within the ψ framework, high <math>\varepsilon_{\rm EM}</math> correlates with high <math>\varepsilon_\psi</math> via the <math>\alpha F_{\mu\nu}F^{\mu\nu}</math> coupling term.
# '''Voltage tunability:''' <math>\varepsilon_r</math> decreases strongly with applied DC electric field — the material is a ferroelectric with field-suppressed polarisation above the Curie point. This makes ε_ψ a controllable parameter rather than a fixed constant.
# '''Room-temperature Curie point at 40% Sr:''' The Ba₀.₆Sr₀.₄TiO₃ composition places the maximum tunability slope <math>d\varepsilon_r/dV</math> in the 15–35°C operating range of the [[HelmKit]].

=== BST Tunability Curve ===

The voltage-dependent relative permittivity of BST at 40 kHz:

