== ψ-Dependent Extensions ==

=== Nonlinear ψ Permittivity ===

At high ψ field amplitudes inside a [[ψ Resonator]] operating near its Q limit, the quadratic and cubic susceptibility terms become relevant:

:<math>\varepsilon_\psi^{\rm eff}(\psi) \approx \varepsilon_\psi^{(1)} + \varepsilon_0\chi_\psi^{(3)}|\psi|^2</math>

A positive <math>\chi_\psi^{(3)}</math> produces self-focusing — the ψ field increases the local permittivity, which slows the wave, which focuses the field further. This is the psionic analogue of the Kerr effect in nonlinear optics and could contribute to soliton formation via a permittivity-mediated rather than purely self-interaction-mediated mechanism.

Whether the BST phase contributes significant <math>\chi_\psi^{(3)}</math> is an open TML-3 question. BST is known to have large electro-optic nonlinearity in the electromagnetic context — a non-zero <math>\chi_\psi^{(3)}</math> would be consistent with this.

=== Operator-State-Dependent ε_ψ ===

Within the Track B framework, if biological cortical tissue has a state-dependent <math>\chi_\psi^{\rm (bio)}(t)</math> (varying with attentional state), then <math>\varepsilon_\psi^{\rm (bio)}(t)</math> varies accordingly. The impedance <math>Z_{\rm bio}(t)</math> therefore shifts with operator state — which is precisely what the Mk2.0 HRV servo estimates via RMSSD and compensates via BST bias tuning.

== TML Measurement Protocol ==

=== TML-1: ε_ψ(V) Curve Measurement ===

'''Instrument:''' Keysight E4990A impedance analyser with 42841A bias adapter (DC bias superimposed on AC measurement signal).

'''Sample preparation:''' Three tablets per batch, 10 mm diameter, 2 mm thick, from full B7+B8 protocol.

'''Protocol:'''
# Deposit 8 mm diameter gold electrodes on both faces by sputter deposition (30 nm Ti adhesion layer + 200 nm Au).
# Mount in coaxial fixture with spring-loaded contacts.
# Sweep DC bias from −200V to +200V in 10V steps. At each step, measure impedance at 40 kHz with 100 mV AC perturbation signal.
# Extract <math>\varepsilon'_\psi(V)</math> and <math>\varepsilon''_\psi(V)</math> from parallel RC equivalent circuit fit.
# Repeat measurement at 1 kHz, 10 kHz, 100 kHz, 1 MHz to characterise frequency dispersion.

'''Pass criteria:'''
* <math>\varepsilon'_\psi(0\,\text{V}) \geq 3.5</math> at 40 kHz (within 15% of Maxwell-Garnett prediction of 4.0)
* Tunability <math>\tau_{\rm eff} = (\varepsilon'(0) - \varepsilon'(200))/\varepsilon'(0) \geq 0.08</math>
* <math>\tan\delta_\psi = \varepsilon''_\psi/\varepsilon'_\psi < 0.01</math> at 40 kHz
* Kramers–Kronig consistency verified by comparing measured <math>\varepsilon'(\omega)</math> against integral of measured <math>\varepsilon''(\omega)</math>

=== TML-3: Biological Cortex ε_ψ Estimation ===

Indirect measurement using cavity resonance frequency shift:

# [[Psi Stabilizer]] shell placed in close proximity to operator's skull (Standby mode — no drive).
# Weak probe signal injected at shell resonant frequency via a secondary coil.
# Resonant frequency <math>\omega_{010}</math> measured via S11 response.
# Repeat in baseline HRV state and peak coherence state (HeartMath coherence breathing, 5 minutes).
# Frequency shift <math>\Delta\omega_{010}</math> between states gives <math>\Delta\varepsilon_\psi^{\rm (bio)}</math> via:

:<math>\frac{\Delta\omega_{010}}{\omega_{010}} \approx -\frac{1}{2}\frac{\Delta\varepsilon_\psi^{\rm (bio)}}{\varepsilon_\psi^{\rm (device)}}\cdot\eta_{\rm fill}</math>

where <math>\eta_{\rm fill}</math> is the filling factor — the fraction of cavity energy stored in the biological tissue region.

'''Sham control required:''' Identical protocol with no operator present (empty cavity). Any frequency drift must be subtracted. See [[Sham Control Design]] (F2).

